WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.130
Site review combines current official airspace information, permissions,

00:00:04.130 --> 00:00:09.115
asset drawings or maps, weather, lighting, terrain, obstacles, electromagnetic conditions,

00:00:09.115 --> 00:00:10.595
and ongoing operations.

00:00:10.595 --> 00:00:14.310
Coordinate with the asset owner and site-safety lead before route design.

00:00:14.310 --> 00:00:15.930
Structures create unique hazards.

00:00:15.930 --> 00:00:19.465
Wires, antennas, cranes, cables, overhangs, moving equipment,

00:00:19.465 --> 00:00:23.800
reflected navigation signals, turbulence, and radio shadowing may be hard to see.

00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:26.910
Obstacle sensing cannot replace standoff and visual awareness.

00:00:26.910 --> 00:00:30.270
Choose launch and recovery areas with stable surfaces, clear access,

00:00:30.270 --> 00:00:34.095
controlled people and vehicles, reliable communication, and emergency options.

00:00:34.095 --> 00:00:37.870
Plan alternate landing areas when the route extends across a large asset.

00:00:37.870 --> 00:00:41.580
The flight path should connect overview coverage with targeted details while preserving

00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:42.395
safe distance.

00:00:42.395 --> 00:00:46.695
Plan camera angles, route direction, altitude strategy, turns, battery segments,

00:00:46.695 --> 00:00:49.000
and areas where the aircraft must not enter.

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:53.540
Crew roles may include pilot in command, visual observer, payload operator, site escort,

00:00:53.540 --> 00:00:55.300
mission lead, and specialist.

00:00:55.300 --> 00:00:59.595
Use concise communication for hazards, asset references, collection status, battery,

00:00:59.595 --> 00:01:01.250
signal, and abort calls.

00:01:01.250 --> 00:01:06.830
Document objective, approvals, crew, aircraft, payload, conditions, route, standoff,

00:01:06.830 --> 00:01:10.655
hazards, controls, emergency actions, data handling, and abort criteria.

00:01:10.655 --> 00:01:14.396
Final go authority remains with the responsible pilot under the operating model.

00:01:14.396 --> 00:01:18.100
Deconflict with maintenance, cranes, lifts, traffic control, rail movement,

00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:20.560
energized work, deliveries, and emergency access.

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:24.800
A route approved on a drawing may be unsafe when simultaneous site activity begins.
